/**
 * @addtogroup Foundation_Workflows_Manual
 * @{
 *
 * This page describes the workflow a developer runs manually from a local
 * checkout. CI should invoke the same repository scripts on the appropriate
 * native or cross-compilation runner; it should not duplicate their CMake
 * sequence in workflow YAML.
 *
 * @section FoundationManualWorkflowModel Execution Model
 *
 * Every command starts with a target preset such as `macos_arm64`,
 * `windows_msvc_x64`, `stm32f407_armgcc_cortex_m4f_hard`, or
 * `atmega328p_avrgcc_avr5`. The preset is the compatibility identity: it selects
 * the platform, compiler/toolchain, architecture, ABI, and generator. Debug and
 * Release are configurations inside that one identity, not separate presets.
 *
 * The scripts use two repository-owned locations:
 *
 * @code
 * build/<preset>/   CMake cache, generated build system, objects, and artifacts
 * dist/<preset>/    Installed package intended for consumption or publication
 * @endcode
 *
 * `build/` is disposable. `dist/` is a package result. Do not publish files
 * directly from the build tree.
 *
 * @section FoundationManualWorkflowPrerequisites Prerequisites
 *
 * From the repository root, first confirm that CMake can see the target preset
 * and the required compiler:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * cmake --version
 * cmake --list-presets=all
 * ninja --version
 * @endcode
 *
 * Ninja is required by the current macOS, Clang, Linux, Arm, and AVR presets.
 * Visual Studio presets instead require the matching Visual Studio installation
 * and should be run from Developer PowerShell. Cross presets additionally need
 * their target compiler and runtime/sysroot. Doxygen is needed only for the
 * documentation workflow.
 *
 * @section FoundationManualWorkflowDaily Daily Development
 *
 * The build script configures before compiling, so a separate configure command
 * is not required for a normal edit/build cycle.
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/build.sh macos_arm64 --config Debug --target Foundation
 * @endcode
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * ./scripts/build.ps1 windows_msvc_x64 `
 *     -Configuration Debug -Target Foundation
 * @endcode
 *
 * Repeating the command is incremental. Use Release explicitly when optimizing
 * or checking the release configuration:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/build.sh macos_arm64 --config Release --target Foundation
 * @endcode
 *
 * Omitting `--target Foundation` or `-Target Foundation` builds the generator's
 * default target and can include optional targets enabled in the current cache.
 * Selecting Debug or Release does not require `--fresh` and does not create a
 * second preset directory.
 *
 * @section FoundationManualWorkflowConfigure Explicit Configuration and Overrides
 *
 * Run configure directly when inspecting the configure summary or selecting a
 * custom feature set:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/configure.sh macos_arm64 --fresh -- \
 *     -DFOUNDATION_FULL_BUILD=OFF \
 *     -DFOUNDATION_SCHEDULING=OFF
 * @endcode
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * ./scripts/configure.ps1 windows_msvc_x64 -Fresh `
 *     -CMakeArguments "-DFOUNDATION_FULL_BUILD=OFF", `
 *                     "-DFOUNDATION_SCHEDULING=OFF"
 * @endcode
 *
 * Cache overrides remain associated with `build/<preset>` until another
 * configure operation replaces them or the cache is refreshed. If an override
 * must apply to an export, pass it to the export command itself; do not rely on
 * an earlier configure call.
 *
 * @section FoundationManualWorkflowFresh Fresh Versus Incremental
 *
 * `--fresh` in Bash and `-Fresh` in PowerShell ask CMake to recreate its cache
 * and generated build-system state. They are optional controls, not part of the
 * target name.
 *
 * | Situation | Fresh? | Reason |
 * | --- | --- | --- |
 * | Rebuild after editing source or headers | No | Incremental compilation is faster. |
 * | Switch between Debug and Release | No | The multi-config tree isolates both configurations. |
 * | Export a local package during iteration | Optional | Explicit command options still apply. |
 * | Create a release candidate | Yes | Begins from preset defaults and declared overrides. |
 * | Change compiler, generator, toolchain, SDK, sysroot, CPU, FPU, or ABI | Yes | Cached detection may no longer be valid. |
 * | Suspect a stale or unexplained option | Yes | Removes hidden cache state. |
 * | Remove the complete build directory | Use clean | Fresh is not a full directory cleanup. |
 *
 * `--fresh` does not delete source files. It is also different from distribution
 * cleanup: export refreshes the default `dist/<preset>` directory itself.
 *
 * @section FoundationManualWorkflowTests Tests and Examples
 *
 * Tests and examples have different purposes:
 *
 * - tests provide pass/fail correctness evidence through CTest;
 * - examples demonstrate public API usage and are not test evidence.
 *
 * Run the native test workflow with:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/test.sh macos_arm64 --config Debug --fresh
 * @endcode
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * ./scripts/test.ps1 windows_msvc_x64 -Configuration Debug -Fresh
 * @endcode
 *
 * The repository currently has no registered CTest cases, so the normal command
 * intentionally fails. `--allow-no-tests` or `-AllowNoTests` may be used only to
 * check that the test configuration compiles while the suite is being created;
 * it is not release evidence. Embedded binaries require target hardware, an
 * emulator, or host-side portable tests as described in
 * @ref Foundation_Workflows_Validation.
 *
 * Examples are excluded from ordinary packages. The export-only opt-in is:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/export.sh macos_arm64 --examples-on
 * @endcode
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * ./scripts/export.ps1 windows_msvc_x64 -ExamplesOn
 * @endcode
 *
 * Native macOS exports also contain the `FoundationExamples.command` launcher,
 * and Windows exports contain `FoundationExamples.cmd`. A developer can
 * double-click it, select any adjacent example, read its exit code, and return
 * to the menu to run another example.
 * Terminal and CI usage should invoke the real executable directly. Distribution
 * packages contain Release examples only; build examples in Debug from the
 * source tree when stepping through their code is useful.
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/build.sh macos_arm64 --config Debug \
 *     --target FoundationExamples --examples-on
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationManualWorkflowInstall Manual Install
 *
 * Install overlays one already-built configuration into a prefix. It does not
 * configure, compile, or remove stale files.
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/build.sh macos_arm64 --config Debug --target Foundation
 * ./scripts/build.sh macos_arm64 --config Release --target Foundation
 * ./scripts/install.sh macos_arm64 --config Debug
 * ./scripts/install.sh macos_arm64 --config Release
 * @endcode
 *
 * This is useful for inspecting CMake installation behavior. For a distributable
 * package, prefer export because it builds both configurations and refreshes the
 * repository-owned destination safely.
 *
 * @section FoundationManualWorkflowExport Release-Candidate Export
 *
 * The normal one-command package excludes examples:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/export.sh macos_arm64 --fresh --parallel 8
 * @endcode
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * ./scripts/export.ps1 windows_msvc_x64 -Fresh -Parallel 8
 * @endcode
 *
 * To publish example executables in the same package, opt in explicitly:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/export.sh macos_arm64 --fresh --examples-on --parallel 8
 * @endcode
 *
 * Feature variants must declare their overrides in the export command:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/export.sh macos_arm64 --fresh -- \
 *     -DFOUNDATION_FULL_BUILD=OFF \
 *     -DFOUNDATION_SCHEDULING=OFF
 * @endcode
 *
 * Place `--examples-on` before the `--` separator when combining it with raw
 * CMake arguments. Export configures once, builds Debug and Release, removes the
 * default `dist/<preset>`, and installs both configurations into that directory.
 * The expected native Unix library pair is `libFoundation_d.a` and
 * `libFoundation.a`; Windows produces `Foundation_d.lib` and `Foundation.lib`.
 * Optional examples are built and installed only for Release.
 *
 * @section FoundationManualWorkflowVerify Verification Before Publication
 *
 * A successful command is necessary but not sufficient. Before publishing:
 *
 * 1. confirm the Debug and Release archives both exist;
 * 2. confirm `include/` and `lib/cmake/Foundation/` exist;
 * 3. inspect `FoundationBuildInfo.cmake` for the platform, compiler, architecture,
 *    feature set, and example selection;
 * 4. inspect archive architecture with the platform tool;
 * 5. compile a separate consumer through `find_package(Foundation CONFIG)`;
 * 6. record the native or embedded validation result for that preset.
 *
 * On macOS, a minimal structural inspection is:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * test -f dist/macos_arm64/lib/libFoundation_d.a
 * test -f dist/macos_arm64/lib/libFoundation.a
 * test -f dist/macos_arm64/lib/cmake/Foundation/FoundationConfig.cmake
 * lipo -archs dist/macos_arm64/lib/libFoundation_d.a
 * lipo -archs dist/macos_arm64/lib/libFoundation.a
 * @endcode
 *
 * Use @ref Foundation_Workflows_Validation for target-specific checks and the
 * release evidence checklist.
 *
 * @section FoundationManualWorkflowDocs Documentation Changes
 *
 * Rebuild documentation whenever a script, option, preset, toolchain, package
 * layout, or workflow changes:
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/docs.sh --fresh
 * @endcode
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * ./scripts/docs.ps1 -Fresh
 * @endcode
 *
 * Generated HTML belongs under `build/documentation/docs/html`; only the Doxygen
 * source files under `docs/` are maintained as repository documentation.
 *
 * @}
 */
